{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792498045000-architect-host-boundary-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md collab/1792502807000-architect-host-boundary-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792498045000-architect-host-boundary-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md collab/1792502807000-architect-host-boundary-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"**NOT SIGNED OFF.** The internal primitive profile is compatible with the doctrine **only when implemented inside the machine**. The proposed migration does not yet establish its claimed complete boundary.\n\n`H` below denotes [dao.stream.host-boundary.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md). I read all 804 lines and the rationale. No files were edited.\n\n| ID | Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | High | H:163–177, 474 | ABI §4 explicitly permits in-instance direct calls; §5 permits internal compiler linkage. §3’s custody rule alone does **not** establish a primitive-profile exception. Frequency and purity cannot determine physical placement: a pure hash imported from the embedding host still crosses the boundary. | Ground the profile in ABI §§4–5. Require every primitive and its libraries to execute in-instance for wasm; prohibit primitive imports and verify the import list against `dao.stream.wasm.md:29–52`. State plainly: porting includes primitives and `main`, so the owner’s literal “only stream implementations” is not achieved. The opening admits this, but §2 softens it. |\n| A2 | Blocking | H:193–208, 560–562 | Passing `random-bytes` or `schedule` functions into portable code is not itself a stream boundary. `dao.jing.content.async:49–57, 87–99` schedules a thunk that re-enters the pump. ABI `:143–148` explicitly rejects both callable host services and relocated callbacks. | Supply entropy as data observed through streams, and have the host driver own pumping/wake handling. Precisely distinguish implementation-private seam calls from application-facing host services. OQ5 authorizes the storage seam, not a general exception to ABI §7. |\n| A3 | Blocking | H:179, 349, 538–540 | Census misses unconditional entropy: `yin/repl.cljc:667–668,733,918`, `yin/vm/linker.cljc:1685`, `dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:27,185`; also `dao/stream/rpc.cljc:112–117` uses `rand`. Thus even “in-memory implementations need no seam” overlooks identity generation. | Inventory resolved calls in the selected closure, including `rand`, UUID creation and ambient properties. Inject identity/entropy inputs. A reader-conditional grep cannot prove absence of world contact. |\n| A4 | High | H:393–436, 580–581 | Profile omits actual carrier dependencies: `dao/data/btree.cljc:103,211–245` uses atomic and weak/soft references; `yin/vm/values.cljc:41–119` implements host equality, hashing and printing; Jing has Rational/Decimal/Float64 carrier implementations. Arrays alone exports 12 functions; integer shim exports 25 before absorbing Jing helpers. | Replace “about 55” with an exact manifest, including runtime prerequisites and explicitly permitted dialect syntax. Assign every residual a slice. Classify regex, formatting, sorting and ordinary collection hashing separately from cryptographic hashing. Scope H0 to the adopted closure and exempt documented syntax, rather than promising no arms across all `src/cljc`. |\n| A5 | High | H:400–425, 504–508 | Integer, CBOR, signature and BLAKE3 fixtures exist. They do not establish a named oracle for every proposed entry. NFC tests inspected cover examples, not a pinned Unicode normalization corpus; byte-copy overlap, UTF-8 failure policy and stable-sort ties lack specified laws here. | Name exact oracle files and cases per entry; label missing suites proposed. Pin Unicode/version semantics, mutation/aliasing rules, malformed-input behavior and sort stability. Preserve documented CBOR corpus exceptions rather than claiming universal parity. |\n| A6 | High | H:546–548, 611–612 | H2 deletes `yin.vm.integer.host`, but consumers include `yin/vm.cljc:32`, `yin/vm/engine.cljc:38`, `yin/vm/values.cljc:30` and three Python tests. The explicit promise that engine remains untouched is false. | Migrate every source/test consumer before deletion; require zero remaining requires. Make H1 exercise new primitives directly—unchanged consumers passing old fixtures proves nothing about the new namespace. |\n| A7 | High | H:365–388 | Storage seam promises portable Node lock discipline, but exposes only `lock!`/`unlock!`. Existing `store/fs.cljc:135–177` needs PID/liveness, exclusive claim creation, unlink and canonical paths. Its placement is unresolved. | Either keep the complete lock algorithm inside the seam and correct the portability claim, or enumerate its lower operations. Specify short reads/writes, partial failure, acquisition and durability outcomes before H6. |\n| A8 | Medium | H:159–160, 564, 584–616 | H4 replaces a writer; H3 replaces Base64; these are not pure code motion. Slowdown factors are unmeasured. Codec disjointness holds only with the stated `.codec` deferral and an explicit file/test allocation. | Remove numerical performance claims or benchmark them; reassess risk and cost after the complete inventory. Keep transport Boring/Dart codec dependencies. Record OQ1–OQ6 as adopted, including arrays staying put. |\n\nJ1 is the principal conflict. J0b/J0c feasibility work can proceed; J0a’s inventory is useful, but its “narrow source adaptations” require file-level coordination.\n\nNot verified:\n\n- Three-lane execution, performance, crash recovery or competing-process behavior.\n- Exhaustive resolved closure coverage or future wasm binaries.\n- Complete oracle coverage for the proposed profile; existing fixtures provide only partial evidence.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 14:05:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 21:05:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nModel: claude-fable-5-1\nSession-ID: 36134bec-9cbd-4852-b78c-4901af66fd02\nTask: revise the dao.stream host-boundary design (r2)\nRole: Architect\nDeliverable: docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md revised in place (r2); no src edits, nothing staged.\nLabels: V verified by reading, R read-not-run, P proposed. Nothing executed. Non-Claude sign-off required again.\n\n# Rationale\n\n**The refined rule changes the basis, not most verdicts.** r1 grounded the\nprimitive profile in \"machine-internal\"; astra's A1 was right that ABI §4-§5\npermit in-instance calls without making purity a criterion. The owner's test 2\nsupplies the criterion: dao.stream wherever the contact *can be an IO action*.\n§2.2 makes that operational with six properties (observation, effect,\nnondeterminism, asynchrony, world-dependent failure, another process), each\ncited to the doctrine. §2.3 decides the borderline cases: fallible-but-pure\ndecoders are direct calls (failure is a function of the input); hashing in a\nlinked library is a direct call that must execute in-instance (wasm: never an\nimport); entropy, time and scheduling are IO; the Python parser is pure\nin-process and IO as another process, and the stage shape serves both. §2.4\nstates plainly where the owner's literal sentence is met (all world contact)\nand not met (the profile and `main` are still per host).\n\n**Entropy was r1's real defect.** Passing `random-bytes`/`schedule` functions\ninto portable code is the \"callable host service\" ABI `:143-148` rejects (A2),\nand the resolved-call census (A3) shows 24 `random-uuid` sites plus `rand`,\n`gensym` and secure generators that a reader-conditional grep cannot see,\nincluding inside the in-memory streams, so \"in-memory needs no seam\" was wrong\nabout identity. r2: an entropy stream the driver deposits onto (events in),\nidentities derived from drawn bytes, `{:identity}` in creation specs, and\n`content.async` turned into a stepped owner the driver pumps. A reproducible-\nidentity test becomes possible for the first time.\n\n**Exactness replaces \"about 55\".** Table A names all 66 functions by name\nand source line; table B the portable replacements; table C the runtime\nprerequisites astra named (btree atomics and weak refs with the strong default,\n`yin.vm.values` and the Jing carriers' equality/hash/print blocks, the promise\nfacade). Oracles are named per entry with V/P labels; laws for aliasing\n(`slice` copies, `copy!` is memmove), malformed UTF-8 (refuse, never U+FFFD),\nUnicode 16.0, and sort stability are pinned.\n\n**Storage seam (A7).** The claim-file algorithm stays portable; the seam\nenumerates the lower operations (`create-exclusive!`, `unlink!`, `list-dir`,\n`own-pid`, `process-alive?`, `canonical-path`) beside the OS lock, with\nshort-read, failed-append, failed-sync and failed-rename outcomes specified.\n\n**Disjointness re-checked (V).** Two one-line edits in codec-track files\n(`codec.cljc` require, `ws.cljc:122` identity) are deferred past C4 and\nrecorded. Nothing else overlaps.\n\n# Disposition of astra's findings\n\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Id | Disposition   | Where in r2                                                                                                    |\n+====+===============+================================================================================================================+\n| A1 | accepted      | §2.1-2.2 basis is purity/totality/synchrony grounded in ABI §4-§5; §7 wasm in-instance rule and import-list    |\n|    |               | gate (H8); §2.4 literal-sentence statement; HD1 supersedes r1's basis.                                         |\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A2 | accepted      | §4.1 seam rule distinguishes implementation-private seams from application-facing services; §4.4 entropy       |\n|    |               | stream, stepped `content.async`, driver-owned pumping, `dao.space.dht` change; H5 rewritten; HD3.              |\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A3 | accepted      | §3.2 resolved-call census (9 rows, every site cited); §3.3 exempt syntax by pattern; H0 scoped to the adopted  |\n|    |               | closure with two lists and two negative tests; in-memory identity claim corrected.                             |\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A4 | accepted      | §4.3 tables A (66 named entries), B, C (atomics, weak/soft refs, carrier types, promise facade); every residual|\n|    |               | assigned a slice or \"none\" with reason; sorting/hashing/regex/format classified in §2.3 and §3.2 row 9.        |\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A5 | accepted      | Oracle files and cases per entry in table A with V/P labels; laws pinned (aliasing, malformed input, Unicode  |\n|    |               | 16.0, stability); corpus errata preserved (table B, H4).                                                       |\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A6 | accepted      | H2 lists all nine consumers incl. the three Python tests; empty-grep gate before deletion; H1 tests the new    |\n|    |               | namespaces directly; \"engine untouched\" withdrawn (§9).                                                        |\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A7 | accepted,     | §4.2: lower operations enumerated (option 2), algorithm stays portable; outcomes for short reads, partial       |\n|    | option 2      | writes, acquisition, durability; H6 acceptance adds scripted-seam tests for each.                               |\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| A8 | accepted      | All slowdown/speedup numbers removed; H4 carries a measurement with no threshold (OQ7); risk and cost           |\n|    |               | reassessed in §8.1 and §9 after the inventory; OQ1-OQ6 recorded as adopted in §11.1 with the owner's quotes.   |\n+----+---------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nNot verified: no lane was run; the §3.2 census is by `rg` over the closure\nwith every hit opened, not by a compiler-resolved call graph; the H4\nperformance question is open until measured.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
