{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '200,218p;351,650p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\"","aggregated_output":"+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Hashing or signing in an external | A deterministic function of bytes (and, for signing, of the seed). The library is linkage (`abi.md`     | Direct     |\n| library (BLAKE3, SHA-256,         | §5). It must execute **in the same instance**: for wasm that means compiled in, never imported (§7),    | call, in-  |\n| Ed25519)                          | because an import is a host crossing and a crossing is a boundary whatever the function's purity (A1).  | instance   |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Key generation, nonces, secrets,  | Entropy: property 3. The portable core never reads a generator; it reads entropy that the driver         | Stream     |\n| fresh identities                  | deposited (§3.2, §4.4).                                                                                 |            |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Time                              | Property 3 and 1. A clock reading is an event the driver deposits or a value it passes as data; the      | Stream /   |\n|                                   | core never calls a clock (V: `dao.stream.ws/accept-connection!` already takes `now`, `ws.cljc:639`).    | data       |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Scheduling, timers, wake-up       | Property 4. The driver owns pumping and wake-up; the core exposes a step the driver calls. A thunk        | Driver     |\n|                                   | handed to a timer that re-enters the core is a relocated callback (`abi.md:146-148`), rejected (A2).     |            |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| The Python parser                 | Two shapes. In-process, synchronous, deterministic over the source text and the pinned grammar: a pure    | Either;    |\n|                                   | function, direct call allowed. As another process or host (property 6): a stream. The stage shape         | the stage  |\n|                                   | (`parser.cljc:65-75`, V) carries both without change: source units in, CST packets out.                   | supports   |\n|                                   |                                                                                                         | both       |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n+---+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n| 8 | Weak/soft refs, atomics        | `dao/data/btree.cljc:103-119,211-245`.                                                                            | C     |\n+---+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n| 9 | `sort`/`sort-by`/`compare`/    | 100+ sites. Byte-ordered where bytes result: `cbor.cljc:1070,1177`, `ucf/rows.cljc:139,181,232-233,305,516`,       | none  |\n|   | `hash`, regex, `format`        | `debruijn.cljc:921,929`, `debruijn_code.cljc:454-459`. The rest order keys by `str`/`pr-str` for determinism of  | (law  |\n|   |                                | iteration and diagnostics (`dht.cljc:909`, `linker.cljc:211,220`, `rows.cljc:581-737`, `repl.cljc:330-340`).       | test) |\n|   |                                | `(format \"` : zero sites in the closure. Regex: Node claim-name parse (`fs.cljc:105`) and frontends. Dialect      |       |\n|   |                                | semantics, §2.3; a cross-lane law test for the byte-ordered sites already exists in their fixtures.               |       |\n+---+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n\nConsequence for r1's claim that in-memory implementations \"need no seam\": they\nneed no **host** seam, but they mint identities (row 1), which is entropy.\nAfter H5 a creation specification carries its identity (`{:identity id}`,\ndata), and the composition mints it from the entropy stream (§4.4).\n\n### 3.3 Exempt dialect syntax (the H0 allowlist's second half)\n\nThese reader-conditional patterns are the dialect's syntax, not host contact,\nand the H0 gate exempts them by pattern, not by file:\n\n- `(catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) ...)` and the\n  `Exception`/`js/Error` variants;\n- type hints `#?(:cljd x :clj ^long x :cljs x)` and `^bytes`, `^String`;\n- protocol and interface names in `deftype` bodies (`cljd.core/IEquiv`,\n  `clojure.lang.IHashEq`, `IPrintWithWriter`) and `print-method`;\n- `exists?`, `js-obj`/`(Object.)` opaque sentinels;\n- `:refer-clojure :exclude` and require-form arms whose only purpose is a\n  namespace alias.\n\nEverything else in a file outside the primitive profile and the seam files is\na gate failure.\n\n## 4. The host-implementation contract\n\n### 4.1 Stream implementations and their seams (IO actions)\n\n+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| Implementation             | Portable core (V)                          | Seam value a host supplies (V today, P as named) | Needed by                                 |\n+============================+============================================+==================================================+===========================================+\n| Ring buffer, memory log,   | `dao.stream.ringbuffer`, `.memory_log`,    | None (host). Identity in the creation spec after | Everything. A pure-evaluator host stops   |\n| remote channel, waitset,   | `.remote_channel`, `.waitset`, `.observe`, | H5.                                              | here.                                     |\n| middleware, apply          | `.middleware`, `.apply`                    |                                                  |                                           |\n+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| WebSocket                  | `dao.stream.ws`                            | `{:connect! :bind! :unbind!}`                    | Network REPL, linker transfer, DHT heads. |\n|                            |                                            | (`yin.repl.host:20-33`); `:connect!` returns     |                                           |\n|                            |                                            | `{:send! :close!}`.                              |                                           |\n+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| Datagram and UDP           | `dao.stream.datagram`, `.udp`              | `bind!` over `{:identity :deposit :bind-host     | DHT, discovery.                           |\n|                            |                                            | :bind-port :max-bytes}` returning `{:send!       |                                           |\n|                            |                                            | :close!}` (`datagram.cljc:15-23`).               |                                           |\n+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| Durable journal, content   | `dao.stream.journal`, `dao.jing.file`,     | P, §4.2.                                         | Durable index store, transactor ledger,   |\n| file and store beneath it  | `dao.space.store.fs`, `.store`,            |                                                  | on-disk content store.                    |\n|                            | `journal.file`, `btree.storage`            |                                                  |                                           |\n+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| Entropy                    | P: `dao.stream.entropy` (a reader over a   | The driver's deposit of CSPRNG bytes (P, §4.4).  | Every composition that mints identities,  |\n|                            | ring buffer; no host code)                 |                                                  | secrets or keys.                          |\n+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| Parser stage               | `yang.antlr.export`, `.packet`,            | P: grammar profile `{:grammar-id :make-lexer     | Python frontend; optionally remote.       |\n|                            | `yang.python.antlr.*`                      | :make-parser :start}` (`cst.clj:9-12`) plus the  |                                           |\n|                            |                                            | host walk, as one seam value.                    |                                           |\n+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n| wasm import surface        | `dao.stream.wasm.md` §1: seven imports.    | The imports themselves. Unchanged.               | wasm instances.                           |\n+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n\nSeam rule (restating `abi.md` §6-§7 as acceptance, and answering A2's\nrequest to distinguish the two function shapes): a seam entry is\n**implementation-private**: it is called only by the stream implementation\nthat owns it, never by application or portable-core code; it takes host\narguments and returns plain data or deposits plain data; it never invokes\napplication code; it classifies host errors into data inside itself; what it\ncannot do it answers as a qualified unsupported outcome. A map of functions\nthat portable application code calls directly (r1's `random-bytes`,\n`schedule`) is an **application-facing host service** and is exactly what\n`abi.md:143-148` rejects; none remains in this design. OQ5 authorizes the\nstorage seam under this rule, not a general exception to §7.\n\n### 4.2 The durable-storage seam (P), with its lower operations\n\nThe claim-file lock algorithm (`store/fs.cljc:111-161`, V) is **kept in\nportable code** and the seam exposes the lower operations it needs, so the\nNode algorithm, the pid-reuse rule and the race argument are written and\ntested once; hosts with an OS lock supply `lock-file!` and declare\n`:lock-kind :os-lock`, hosts without supply the claim-file primitives and\ndeclare `:claim-file`. Every entry answers a map under `:dao.host.storage/`;\nno entry throws into framing or lock code.\n\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Entry                   | Today (V)                                                                  | Outcome data and law                                          |\n+=========================+============================================================================+===============================================================+\n| `open!` path mode       | `file.cljc:159-178`; `fs.cljc:198-208`                                     | `{:ok handle}` / `{:failed reason}`. Creates parents when      |\n|                         |                                                                            | asked.                                                        |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `length` handle         | `file.cljc:181-185`                                                        | `{:ok n}` / `{:failed}`.                                      |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `read-at` handle off n  | `file.cljc:188-213`                                                        | `{:ok bytes}` with exactly `n` bytes, or `{:short bytes}`      |\n|                         |                                                                            | when fewer exist (the torn-tail signal F3 needs), or           |\n|                         |                                                                            | `{:failed}`. Never a partial buffer without the `:short` tag. |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `append!` handle bytes  | `file.cljc:223-243`                                                        | `{:ok}` only when every byte is written; otherwise `{:failed}` |\n|                         |                                                                            | with `:written` unknown. The portable layer treats `:failed`  |\n|                         |                                                                            | as a possibly torn tail and relies on replay truncation (F3). |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `truncate!` handle n    | `file.cljc:216-220`                                                        | `{:ok}` / `{:failed}`.                                        |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `sync!` handle          | `file.cljc:246-252`                                                        | `{:ok}` (durable per the seam's declared failure model) /     |\n|                         |                                                                            | `{:failed}` (not durable; the put is not acknowledged, F1).   |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `close!` handle         | `file.cljc:255-259`                                                        | `{:ok}`; idempotent.                                          |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `lock-file!` path       | `fs.cljc:198-223` (JVM `tryLock`, Dart `lockSync`)                         | `{:held release}` / `{:refused}` / `{:unsupported}`. Present   |\n|                         |                                                                            | iff `:lock-kind :os-lock`.                                    |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `create-exclusive!`     | `fs.cljc:142` (`openSync \"wx\"`)                                            | `{:ok}` / `{:exists}` / `{:failed}`. Present iff `:lock-kind   |\n| path                    |                                                                            | :claim-file`.                                                 |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `unlink!` path          | `fs.cljc:140,149`                                                          | `{:ok}` / `{:absent}` / `{:failed}`.                           |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `list-dir` path         | `fs.cljc:108`, `store.cljc:105`, `journal/file.cljc:58`                    | `{:ok names}` / `{:failed}`.                                  |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `own-pid`,              | `fs.cljc:92,136`                                                           | `{:ok pid}`; `{:ok true|false}` with `EPERM` read as alive;    |\n| `process-alive?` pid    |                                                                            | present iff `:claim-file`.                                    |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `canonical-path` path   | `fs.cljc:171-177`                                                          | `{:ok path}` / `{:failed}`.                                   |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `write-text-atomically!`| `fs.cljc:352-378` (temp, fsync, rename)                                    | `{:ok}` / `{:failed stage}` where stage names write, sync or  |\n| dir name text           |                                                                            | rename; a `:failed` leaves HEAD as it was (rename atomicity). |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `sync-dir!` path        | `fs.cljc:292-314`                                                          | `{:ok}` / `{:failed}` / `{:unsupported}`; declared as           |\n|                         |                                                                            | `:directory-sync?` in the seam's capability map.              |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `read-text` path        | `fs.cljc:77-84`, `store.cljc:159`                                          | `{:ok text}` / `{:absent}` / `{:failed}`.                      |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `ensure-dir!` path      | `journal/file.cljc:55-61`                                                  | `{:ok}` / `{:failed}`.                                        |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Capability map          | `journal/file.cljc:36-41` (durability declaration)                         | `{:lock-kind :os-lock|:claim-file, :directory-sync? bool,      |\n|                         |                                                                            | :failure-model :process-crash|:power-loss}`.                  |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nPortable and written once: framing and torn-tail recovery (F1-F6), the\nclaim-file algorithm and its pid-reuse refusal, the same-process `held`\nrecord, the HEAD protocol, replay order. The temp-file name's nonce\n(`fs.cljc:351`) and the claim nonce (`:137`) come from the entropy stream\n(§4.4), not from `random-uuid`.\n\n### 4.3 The primitive profile: exact manifest\n\n`dao.host.*` (OQ2 adopted) is a namespace family whose files contain only\nreader-conditional entries and docstrings stating each entry's law and\noracle. **Table A** is the profile; **table B** the portable code that\nreplaces host libraries; **table C** the runtime prerequisites a dialect must\nprovide, which are not functions and not a boundary but are part of what a\nport verifies.\n\n**Table A: profile entries (P), 66 functions.**\n\n+--------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Namespace          | Entries (consolidated from, V)                                                | N   | Oracle (V = exists; P = proposed suite)                    |\n+====================+===============================================================================+=====+============================================================+\n| `dao.host.integer` | From `yin.vm.integer.host` (all 25): `big-carrier?`, `exact-integer?`,        | 28  | V `test/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc` (26 deftests),           |\n|                    | `safe-native?`, `host-float?`, `nan-float?`, `infinite-float?`, `promote`,    |     | `integer_v3_fixtures.cljc`; V `test/yang/python/antlr/`    |\n|                    | `from-native`, `demote`, `to-native`, `native->double`, `trunc-native`,       |     | `int_contract_fixtures.cljc` and `test/resources/yang/`    |\n|                    | `add`, `sub`, `mul`, `quot-trunc`, `rem-trunc`, `compare-big`, `bit-and-big`, |     | `python/int-contract-v1.txt` (CPython-generated); V the    |\n|                    | `bit-or-big`, `bit-xor-big`, `shift-left-big`, `shift-right-big`,             |     | cbor corpus integer cases (tags 2/3, magnitude bytes). Laws|\n|                    | `magnitude-bit-length`, `to-radix-string`; plus from `cbor.cljc:106-254`:     |     | as in `host.cljc` docstrings; `parse-decimal` refuses      |\n|                    | `parse-decimal` (string to big), `gcd`, `magnitude-bytes`.                    |     | non-digit input with a qualified refusal.                  |\n+--------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.float`   | `float64->bits` (16 hex), `bits->float64`, `finite?`, `integral?`             | 5   | V cbor corpus float cases incl. NaN canonical bits         |\n|                    | (`cbor.cljc:525-560`, `debruijn.cljc:855-885`, `data.cljc:77-92`),            |     | `7ff8000000000000`; V `debruijn_test` scalar cases. Law:   |\n|                    | `fields` (sign, biased exponent, fraction).                                   |     | `bits->float64` of `float64->bits` is identity for every   |\n|                    |                                                                               |     | non-NaN; every NaN maps to the canonical bits.             |\n+--------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.bytes`   | `bytes?`, `alloc`, `length`, `get`, `set!`, `slice`, `copy!`, `equal?`,       | 10  | P `dao.host.bytes-test`: round-trip and slice laws,        |\n|                    | `from-vector`, `to-vector` (from `chunks.cljc:6-32`, `jing/dht.cljc:100-121`, |     | overlap cases. Laws: `slice` always copies (no aliasing);  |\n|                    | `linker.cljc:1232`, `sign.cljc:55-59`, `codec.cljc:45`, `boring.cljc:46-51`,  |     | `copy!` with overlapping source and target behaves as       |\n|                    | `datagram.cljc:63-66`).                                                       |     | memmove; indices out of range refuse, never read garbage.  |\n+--------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.text`    | `unit-count`, `code-unit`, `units->string`, `utf8-encode`, `utf8-decode`,     | 6   | V `test/yin/vm/data_test.cljc` code-point cases; V cbor    |\n|                    | `nfc` (from `data.cljc:119-173`, `debruijn.cljc:241,310-321`, `jing.cljc`,    |     | corpus `unpaired-surrogate` refusals; P `dao.host.text-    |\n|                    | `sign.cljc:62-66`).                                                           |     | test` with a pinned subset of Unicode 16.0                  |\n|                    |                                                                               |     | `NormalizationTest.txt` (the version is already owner-      |\n|                    |                                                                               |     | settled, `debruijn.cljc:314`). Laws: `utf8-decode` refuses |\n|                    |                                                                               |     | invalid sequences and unpaired surrogates with a qualified |\n|                    |                                                                               |     | refusal, never substitutes U+FFFD; `utf8-encode` of a      |\n|                    |                                                                               |     | string with an unpaired surrogate refuses.                 |\n+--------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.hash`    | `blake3-bytes`, `sha256-bytes` (`jing.cljc:59-135`; string forms become      | 2   | V `test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,         |\n|                    | portable over `text`).                                                        |     | `test/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn`, every address  |\n|                    |                                                                               |     | in `cbor-v1.json`; P official BLAKE3 and NIST SHA-256       |\n|                    |                                                                               |     | vectors added to `dao.host.hash-test`.                      |\n+--------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.sign`    | `keypair-from-seed`, `sign`, `verify` (`sign.cljc`; hex, domains, message     | 3   | V `test/yin/vm/linker/sign_test.cljc` (5 deftests),        |\n|                    | assembly stay portable).                                                      |     | `sign_vectors.edn` (RFC 8032 and envelope vectors).         |\n+--------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.data.arrays`  | Stays in place (OQ2): `anew`, `aget`, `aset`, `alength`, `aclone`, `acopy`,   | 12  | V `test/dao/data/btree_test.cljc`, `psset_fixtures.cljc`;   |\n| (profile member    | `asub`, `aconcat`, `asort`, `ainsert-at`, `aremove-at`, `from-coll`           |     | P `asort` stability fixture (equal keys keep input order)  |\n| by reference)      | (`arrays.cljc:28-182`).                                                       |     | after `parallelSort` is replaced by a stable sort.          |\n+--------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n**Table B: portable over the profile, no host arm (P).** Base64 encode and\ndecode (replaces `goog.crypt.base64`, `dart:convert`, `java.util.Base64`;\noracle V `test/dao/stream/base64_test` if present, else P, plus RFC 4648\nvectors); the Jing CBOR writer (replaces Boring and the Dart `cbor` package\non the content path only; oracle V `cbor-v1.json`, `cbor_fixtures.cljc`,\n`cbor_fixtures_test.cljc`, `cbor_conformance_test.cljc`, byte-identical,\n**with the corpus's recorded errata preserved**, `cbor-v1.errata.md`, V);\nhex; `chunks/split` and `chunks/absorb` over `bytes`.\n\n**Table C: runtime prerequisites (documented, verified by the existing\nsuites, not profile functions).**\n\n+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n| Prerequisite                     | Where, and the law a port must meet (V)                                                             | Slice |\n+==================================+=====================================================================================================+=======+\n| `deftype` with host equality,    | `yin/vm/values.cljc:39-119` (`Closure`, `Continuation`: equal iff owner and payload equal; hash of  | H1    |\n| hashing and printing protocols   | `[:tag owner payload]`; prints the marker text); `dao/jing/cbor.cljc:342-460` (`Float64`: equal by | (doc) |\n|                                  | bits with NaN equal to NaN; `Rational`, `Decimal`: equal by fields; hashes as named at `:372-444`).  |       |\n+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n| Atomic flag                      | `btree.cljc:87-119`: transient ownership token readable across threads.                             | none  |\n+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n| Weak and soft references         | `btree.cljc:182-245`: optional node-cache modes; `:strong` default (owner-adopted) so a host without | none  |\n|                                  | them declares the modes unsupported and correctness is unchanged.                                   |       |\n+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n| `locking` (JVM), atoms,          | Five `locking` sites (§3.2 row 7); single-owner discipline elsewhere.                               | none  |\n| volatiles                        |                                                                                                     |       |\n+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n| Host promise type                | `btree/storage.cljc:218-227`: becomes driver-side composition code in H5 (table 3.1).              | H5    |\n+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n| Dialect syntax                   | §3.3.                                                                                               | H0    |\n+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n\nLayering: `dao.host.*` requires nothing in the repo. `dao.jing`,\n`dao.jing.cbor`, `yin.vm.integer`, `yin.vm.data`, `yin.vm.values`,\n`yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm` and the three Python integer tests require it;\n`yin.vm.integer.host` is deleted only when `grep yin.vm.integer.host src/\ntest/` is empty (H2; review A6). `dao` never requires `yin`.\n\n### 4.4 Entropy, clock and scheduling as stream data; the driver\n\n**Entropy (P).** One `dao.stream.entropy` reader: a ring-buffer stream the\ndriver's seam deposits CSPRNG bytes onto, in fixed-size batches, and refills\nwhen a lifecycle event reports consumption. Portable code holds a cursor on\nit like any stream and draws bytes with `next`; an empty entropy stream is\n`:dao.stream/blocked`, answered as data, never waited on. Fresh identities,\nsecrets, nonces, request ids and key seeds are **derived from drawn bytes**\nby a portable function (`dao.stream.entropy/fresh-id`, a hex string of 16\ndrawn bytes). Creation specifications carry `{:identity id}`; `create!` no\nlonger mints. In tests the \"driver\" is a fixture that deposits a fixed byte\nsequence, which makes identities reproducible, a property no test has today.\nThis is \"events in\" (`abi.md` §6): the host initiates nothing and invokes\nnothing; the deposit is the adapter's. No portable code calls a generator.\n\n**Clock.** Unchanged: a reading is data the driver passes (`now`) or deposits\n(a tick event). The contract lists it as the driver's duty.\n\n**Scheduling (P, review A2).** `dao.jing.content.async` loses\n`default-schedule` and its self-rescheduling pump. `async-content` becomes a\nstepped owner with a `step!` the driver calls on its own cadence (the\n`waitset.driver` sleep/nudge pattern, `driver.clj:1-14`, V, already models\nthis); callbacks owed to consumers fire inside `step!`. `btree.storage`'s\npromise facade (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`) moves to driver-side\ncomposition code that steps and resolves, or its consumers step directly;\nthe portable namespace keeps only the stepped functions. `dao.space.dht`\ndrops `secure-random-bytes` and reads the entropy stream for node ids and\nnonces; `default-bind` stays a seam selection (the datagram seam value the\ncomposition passes).\n\n**The driver (`main`)**, per host, supplies: the seam values of §4.1, the\nentropy deposit, clock readings, the pump cadence, stdin/stdout, process exit,\nand ambient facts as data (`:host-banner`, `:home-dir`). Not a contract of\nthis document beyond that list.\n\n### 4.5 Relation to the conformance kit and the wasm surface\n\n`yin.vm.engine.md` §E.5 is engine conformance; it presupposes a correct host\nvalue model and correct stream adapters. This document supplies both suites:\n\n1. **Primitive-profile conformance** (`dao.host.conformance-test`, P): every\n   table A oracle on every lane. E.5.2's \"R, owner, seal, profile and manifest\n   addresses bit-identical\" is downstream of it.\n2. **Stream-implementation conformance** (`dao.stream.conformance-test`, P):\n   the contract's exhaustive outcome tables through a scripted seam, for every\n   implementation. E.5.3's W0 adapter probes are this suite instantiated for\n   wasm.\n\n## 5. What porting a host consists of\n\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| #   | Step                                                                  | Size and proof                                                             |\n+=====+=======================================================================+============================================================================+\n| 1   | Implement table A (66 functions) and meet table C for the dialect.    | Green `dao.host.conformance-test`. Hash and signature bind a library       |\n|     |                                                                       | compiled into the same instance; the rest is the dialect's own values.     |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 2   | Load the adopted closure with a reader feature only where §3.3 syntax | Zero source edits outside `dao.host.*`; the H0 gate proves it.             |\n|     | requires it.                                                          |                                                                            |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 3   | Pure-evaluator milestone: in-memory streams, entropy fixture, local   | The `yin.vm` and `yin.repl` suites minus network and storage; §E.5.        |\n|     | REPL.                                                                 |                                                                            |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 4   | Seam values per milestone: entropy deposit, storage (§4.2), datagram, | Each about 100-350 lines today (V); green `dao.stream.conformance-test`,   |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
