 lanes with recorded test\nand assertion counts; \"unchanged\" means equal counts plus any added test\nnamed. The slow tier runs once at each phase boundary.\n\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Slice | Phase  | Change                                                                         | Falsifiable acceptance                                            |\n+=======+========+================================================================================+===================================================================+\n| H0    | freeze | No `src/` change. (a) A committed list of the adopted closure's namespaces;    | Gate passes on the current tree with exactly the §1 and §3.2      |\n|       |        | (b) a gate test over it with two lists: files permitted host arms (today's §1  | lists; fails on one injected `#?(:clj` outside the list and on    |\n|       |        | table) and resolved effect calls permitted (today's §3.2 sites), exempting the | one injected `(random-uuid)` (two negative tests). Counts         |\n|       |        | §3.3 patterns; (c) per-lane counts; (d) the corpus hash set.                   | recorded.                                                         |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H1    | expand | Create `dao.host.integer`, `.float`, `.bytes`, `.text` with today's arms moved | Green; counts unchanged plus the named new tests; the new tests    |\n|       |        | verbatim, **plus direct tests of each new namespace** (`dao.host.*-test`,      | call the new namespaces directly (review A6), including the       |\n|       |        | table A oracles). Table C laws written into the docstrings of `values.cljc`    | proposed byte and text suites. Corpus bytes identical.            |\n|       |        | and the Jing carriers. No consumer changes.                                    |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H2    | migr.  | Every consumer of `yin.vm.integer.host` moves: `yin/vm.cljc:32`,               | Green; counts unchanged; corpus identical; `grep yin.vm.integer.  |\n|       |        | `yin/vm/engine.cljc:38`, `yin/vm/values.cljc:30`, `yin/vm/data.cljc:41`,       | host src/ test/` empty **before** the deletion commit; `grep      |\n|       |        | `yin/vm/integer.cljc:101`, `test/yang/python/antlr/{int_heap,int_literal,      | BigInteger src/cljc` hits only `dao/host/integer.cljc`.           |\n|       |        | int_ops}_test.cljc` (V); `dao.jing.cbor` drops `:106-254`; `chunks`,           | `yin.vm.engine` **is** touched (its require); its behaviour is    |\n|       |        | `jing.dht`, `linker`, `sign`, `datagram`, `data`, `debruijn` read `bytes`/     | pinned by the engine suites.                                      |\n|       |        | `text`. Then `yin.vm.integer.host` deleted.                                    |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H3    | expand | `dao.host.hash`, `dao.host.sign`; `dao.jing` and `yin.vm.linker.sign` require  | Green; counts unchanged plus the vector tests; every address in   |\n|       | +migr. | them. Base64 made portable (table B).                                          | the corpus identical; `sign_vectors.edn` passes; base64 RFC 4648  |\n|       |        |                                                                                | vectors pass on three lanes; `grep MessageDigest\\|Blake3\\|noble\\| |\n|       |        |                                                                                | ed25519 src/cljc` hits only `dao/host/`.                          |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H4    | migr.  | Portable Jing CBOR writer; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` and `dao.jing.cbor.cljd`     | Green; `cbor-v1.json` (errata preserved), fixtures and             |\n|       |        | deleted; Boring stays for `dao.stream.cbor.boring` (OQ4 adopted). Includes a   | conformance byte-identical on three lanes; DHT and linker image   |\n|       |        | **measurement**: encode throughput of the corpus and of one large module       | goldens unchanged; the measurement recorded in the slice report   |\n|       |        | image, old writer against new, on the JVM and Node.                            | with no threshold claimed here; the owner reads it (OQ7).         |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H5    | expand | Entropy, scheduling, ambient facts, printing (§3.2 rows 1-3, 5-7): the entropy | Green; counts unchanged plus the entropy fixture tests; a test    |\n|       | +migr. | stream and `fresh-id`; `{:identity}` in creation specs; the 24 `random-uuid`   | runs the REPL composition twice over the same entropy fixture     |\n|       |        | sites, `rand` sites, `gensym` sites and the secure generators migrated;        | and asserts identical identities; `grep 'random-uuid\\|(rand\\|     |\n|       |        | `content.async` stepped; `btree.storage` promise facade moved driver-side;     | gensym\\|SecureRandom\\|getRandomValues\\|setTimeout\\|Timer' ` over  |\n|       |        | `repl.cljc:275` banner and `fs.cljc:271` platform as supplied data; the one    | the closure hits only `yin/repl/main.cljc` and the seam files.    |\n|       |        | `println`. **`ws.cljc:122` is deferred** until the codec track's C4 has        |                                                                   |\n|       |        | landed (file-disjointness, §8.1).                                              |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|       |        | **Phase A boundary: slow tier once.**                                          |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H6    | expand | The storage seam (§4.2) per host under `src/clj`, `src/cljs`, `src/cljd`;      | Green; `file_test` (18), `fs_test` (4), btree durability, journal |\n|       | +migr. | `dao.jing.file`, `store.fs`, `store`, `journal.file`, `btree.storage` portable | and transactor suites unchanged in count and result; the          |\n|       |        | over a seam value; claim-file algorithm portable over the lower operations.    | claim-file race test (`fs_test` `claimed` hook, V `fs.cljc:233`)  |\n|       |        | Split per namespace, `jing.file` first.                                        | passes over the seam; kill/restart fixtures pass; short-read and  |\n|       |        |                                                                                | failed-sync outcomes each have a scripted-seam test.               |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H7    | expand | Parser stage as a declared seam value; a test composes the stage on one side   | Green; counts unchanged plus the remote-stage test; packet         |\n|       |        | of an in-memory stream and lowering on the other.                              | goldens unchanged.                                                |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H8    | expand | `dao.stream.conformance-test` over a scripted seam against every               | Green; one named suite; each implementation's declared outcome    |\n|       |        | implementation; the wasm import-list gate (§7); a pointer in                   | subset asserted; the import gate has one negative test.            |\n|       |        | `yin.vm.engine.md` §E.5.                                                       |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|       |        | **Phase B boundary: slow tier once; the H0 lists are now `dao/host/*`, the     |                                                                   |\n|       |        | seam files, `yin/repl/main.cljc` and §3.3 patterns only.**                     |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nDependencies: H1 before H2; H2 before H3, H4, H5 (bytes); H5 before H6\n(nonces); H7 and H8 after H0 only.\n\n### 8.1 File-disjointness from the codec track (re-checked, V)\n\nThe codec track's C2-C4 edit `dao/stream/ws.cljc`, `transit.cljc`,\n`cbor.cljc`, `codec.cljc`, `remote_channel.cljc`, `jing/stream.cljc`,\n`data.cljc`, the four ws adapters, `yin/repl/{serve,connect}.cljc` and their\ntests (`codec.md` §7.1-7.2). H0-H8 touch none of these except: (i) H2's one\nrequire in `dao.stream.codec` (`host-bytes?` over `dao.host.bytes`), deferred\npast C4; (ii) H5's `ws.cljc:122` identity site, deferred past C4. Both are\none-line edits recorded as the track's debt to itself. `dao.stream.chunks`,\n`datagram.cljc`, `remote.cljc`, `remote_pair.cljc`, `remote_route.cljc`,\n`journal.cljc` are not codec-track files. The transport codec keeps Boring\nand the Dart CBOR package (OQ4).\n\nRisk, reassessed after the inventory: H1-H3 low (code motion behind byte\noracles; the ClojureDart traps are named in §13). H4 low-to-medium (the\ncorpus decides; performance unknown until measured). H5 medium: 30-odd sites\nacross stream, linker, REPL and DHT namespaces, and the stepped rewrite of\n`content.async`; mitigated by the reproducible-identity test. H6 medium:\nthe storage namespaces are the least tested across hosts (R) and the\nclaim-file algorithm must keep its race argument over the seam.\n\n## 9. Cost and what would reverse this\n\nCost, orders of magnitude (P, from §1 and §3.2): phase A edits about 35\nportable files (H2 nine, H3 three, H4 three, H5 about twenty) and adds six\n`dao.host.*` files and five test namespaces; phase B edits five storage files\nand adds three seam files and two suites. `yin.vm.semantic.*`,\n`dao.space.index`, `dao.space.query` and the UCF are untouched;\n`yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm` and `yin.vm.values` are touched by a require (H2).\nTests: about 10^3 rerun per lane (R: JVM quick tier about 4,000; `dao.stream`\nand `dao.jing` subsystems about 690 per lane), about 10^1 rewritten (those\nthat construct streams, sign keys or storage directly, and `integer_test`'s\nshim cases), about 10^1 added. No performance figure is claimed; H4 measures.\n\nWhat would reverse the design:\n- An owner ruling that reader-conditional arms are the intended port\n  mechanism. Cheaper per port, no enumeration, no oracle per entry, a\n  library requirement per host.\n- A measured H4 regression that matters. The portable writer stays as the\n  oracle and a host supplies a faster one behind the same profile.\n- A host whose lock or durability semantics the §4.2 seam cannot express as\n  data: it declares the capability absent and compositions refuse durable\n  storage there; the seam shape stands.\n- A decision that identities may stay host-minted. Then H5 shrinks to the\n  secure-generator sites and reproducible identities are not promised.\n- Owner rejection of the §12 amendment: then §2 stands as this document's\n  reading of the existing ABI text and the ABI is not changed.\n\n## 10. Open owner decisions\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| Id   | Question                                                                                             | Architect's recommendation (not a decision)        |\n+======+======================================================================================================+====================================================+\n| OQ7  | H4's measurement: what regression, if any, is acceptable for the portable Jing writer against Boring | Accept any regression under the corpus' identity;  |\n|      | on the JVM and Node.                                                                                 | if the owner wants a bound, set it after H4's      |\n|      |                                                                                                      | numbers exist, not before.                         |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ8  | Approve the proposed ABI amendment (§12) recording the IO-action test, and the one-sentence change   | Approve; §12 is the owner's rule in the owner's    |\n|      | to `datom.world.md:70`.                                                                              | words plus the operational definition of §2.2.     |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ9  | Entropy shape: the stream of §4.4 (recommended) or identity-in-spec only, with secrets and seeds      | The stream; it is the only shape under which the   |\n|      | still host-minted in the driver.                                                                     | core calls no generator and tests are reproducible.|\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ10 | Whether `gensym` in the Clojure and PHP compilers is worth threading a counter through compiler      | Yes, in H5; it is three sites and makes compiler   |\n|      | state (reproducible output) or stays an accepted residual.                                           | output reproducible.                               |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n\nOQ1-OQ6 are closed (§11.1).\n\n## 11. Decision record\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| Id   | Decision                                                                                                         | Status                       |\n+======+==================================================================================================================+==============================+\n| HD1  | The governing test: dao.stream wherever the contact can be an IO action (§2.2); a pure, total, synchronous       | Owner rule, 2026-10-20       |\n|      | same-host function may be a direct call. Supersedes r1's \"machine-internal\" basis (review A1).                   | (\"test 2 governs\")           |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD2  | Pure contacts form the primitive profile, an exact manifest (§4.3 table A, 66 entries) plus documented runtime   | Architect r2; needs non-     |\n|      | prerequisites (table C); for wasm they execute in-instance and are never imported (§7).                          | Claude sign-off              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD3  | Entropy, clock, scheduling and ambient facts are IO actions: supplied as stream data or driver-passed data; no   | Architect r2 (review A2)     |\n|      | callable host service or relocated callback in portable code (§4.4).                                             |                              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD4  | Storage is a seam beneath the journal with enumerated lower operations; the claim-file algorithm stays           | Architect r2 (review A7);    |\n|      | portable (§4.2).                                                                                                 | OQ5 adopted                  |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD5  | Migration H0-H8 (§8); H0 gates both reader arms and resolved effect calls over the adopted closure; file-        | Architect r2                 |\n|      | disjoint from the codec track with two deferred one-line edits (§8.1); H4 measures rather than claims.            |                              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n\n### 11.1 Owner decisions recorded (2026-10-20)\n\nThe owner adopted all six r1 recommendations: \"agree with all of fable's\nrecommendations\"; and for OQ5 separately: \"makes sense. i agree with fable's\nrecommendation\".\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| Id   | Decision as adopted                                                                                              | Status                       |\n+======+==================================================================================================================+==============================+\n| OQ1  | Scope is the `yin.repl` closure; GUI, graphics and demo namespaces excluded.                                     | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| OQ2  | The profile family is `dao.host.*`; `dao.data.arrays` stays in place as a profile member by reference.           | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| OQ3  | Arm-adding work in the files H1-H3 touch is paused until phase A lands; boundary-agnostic probes proceed.         | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| OQ4  | Boring and the Dart `cbor` package are dropped from the Jing content path only; the transport codec keeps them.  | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| OQ5  | Storage is the seam of §4.2, not a streams-all-the-way-down redesign.                                            | Owner, 2026-10-20 (\"makes    |\n|      |                                                                                                                  | sense. i agree ...\")         |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| OQ6  | This track has its own orchestrator seat, independent of the codec track and any host-lane track.                | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n\n## 12. PROPOSED AMENDMENT to datom.world.abi.md (for the owner's approval; not applied)\n\nThis section is a proposal. Nothing in `datom.world.abi.md` or\n`datom.world.md` has been edited. If approved (OQ8), the orchestrator applies\nit as its own docs commit.\n\n**12.1 `datom.world.abi.md`, new §8 \"The governing test\", appended after §7.**\n\n> ## 8. The governing test\n>\n> **Owner ruling, 2026-10-20 (verbatim):** \"when the host boundary can be an\n> IO monad then dao.stream is used. If it cannot be a monad than dao.stream\n> doesnt have to be used\". Asked whether same-host-ness or the IO-action test\n> governs, the owner answered \"yes, test 2 governs\".\n>\n> A host contact uses `dao.stream` if and only if it can be an IO action: its\n> result depends on something other than its arguments; it changes something\n> outside the machine's values; two equal calls may answer differently\n> (entropy, a clock, a process counter); its answer is not available when the\n> call returns; whether it completes depends on the world; or it runs in\n> another process, host or trust domain. A same-host effect (a file read, a\n> clock reading, entropy) is an IO action and so a stream.\n>\n> A contact that cannot be an IO action, because it is a pure, total,\n> synchronous function of its arguments executed inside the same instance,\n> may be a direct call. Such functions are the dialect's value model and the\n> deterministic functions over it (exact integers, bytes, text, hashing,\n> signing, canonical encoding). They are enumerated in a host primitive\n> profile with an oracle per entry ([dao.stream.host-boundary.md](./dao.stream.host-boundary.md)\n> §4.3), execute in-instance (for wasm: compiled in, never imported, §1 of\n> [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md)), and add no contract to\n> `dao.stream`. Linking a library to compute one is internal linkage (§5).\n>\n> §7's two non-adapters are read under this test: a map of functions that\n> portable application code calls to reach the world is a host service and is\n> rejected; a seam a stream implementation calls privately to perform its own\n> IO is that implementation's adapter and is not.\n\n**12.2 `datom.world.md:70`, one sentence replaced.**\n\nToday: \"A host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary;\nimplementations do not add contracts.\"\n\nProposed: \"A host boundary that can be an IO action is the dao.stream\nabstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts. A pure, total,\nsynchronous host function is a primitive-profile entry, not a boundary\n([datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §8).\"\n\n## 13. For a successor orchestrator\n\nDecided (HD1-HD5, OQ1-OQ6): the governing test, the exact profile, the\nentropy and scheduling shape, the storage seam with lower operations, the\nslice sequence. Open: OQ7-OQ10; none blocks H0-H4. OQ9 should be settled\nbefore H5; OQ8 is independent of every slice.\n\nStart with **H0**: it changes no source and records both lists the later\ngates shrink. Then H1 (new files and their direct tests only). H2 is the\nfirst deletion; its gate is the empty grep before the deletion commit. Run\nphase A to the slow tier before H6.\n\nLanes and gates (`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`, Commands and Gate Ladder):\niterate on the JVM lane with `bb test:sub:clj dao.jing` or `yin.vm` (G0); at\neach checkpoint the three-lane subsystem test (G1); before landing\n`bb test:changed` across all three lanes in the worktree (G2); `bb test` on\nmaster after merge (G3). Record per-lane counts before and after every slice.\nThe Dart lane needs `flutter` on PATH; a fresh worktree needs `npm ci`.\nClojureDart traps for exactly this kind of file: `:cljd` first in every mixed\nconditional (the host-eval pass also matches `:clj`); a tail-position\n`:cljd nil` silently fails; protocol method parameters never `[_ _]`;\n`(- 0.0)` is `+0.0`; multi-key `assoc` on nil misbehaves.\n\nReview requirement: this design and any implementation an orchestrator\nderives from it under a Claude model are Claude-authored. **Sign-off on this\nr2 and on each slice must come from a non-Claude architect**; a Claude\nreviewer may review but may not be the sole sign-off. The reviewer checks, in\norder: that every contact classified \"direct call\" in §3 satisfies §2.2 (none\nof the six properties); that no slice deletes an arm or namespace a later\nslice still reads, and H2's grep is empty before its deletion commit; that\nH1-H4 acceptance is byte-identical against the named corpus with errata\npreserved; that H5 leaves no callable generator or timer in the closure; that\nH6 keeps the claim-file race argument over the seam; that nothing edits a\ncodec-track file while C2-C4 are in flight except the two recorded deferrals.\n\n## Appendix A. Citations opened for this document (2026-10-20, read only)\n\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Cite                                                         | Finding                                                                                          |\n+==============================================================+==================================================================================================+\n| `docs/design/datom.world.md:16-32,43-48,68-71`               | Axioms; \"Side effects must appear as stream emissions\"; the sentence §12.2 amends.               |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/datom.world.abi.md:21-24,35-44,47-52,56-64,`    | One ABI; custody; machine-internal work in-instance (§4); the floor and internal linkage (§5);   |\n| `75-87,94-148`                                               | effects out, events in; adapter discipline; `:143-148` the two non-adapters.                     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:29-52,96-105`                | Seven imports; \"primitive application ... no imports\"; Jing bytes.                               |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:707-894`                       | E.5 method; E.5.2 gates; E.5.3 W0 probe list.                                                    |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/dao.stream.codec.md:140-180,658-750,752-780`    | Contract namespace; slice file set; test namespaces (the §8.1 disjointness list).                |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/integer/host.cljc:1-298`                    | 25 public functions (names at `:41-293`); \"depends on nothing\".                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:1-101`; `yin/vm.cljc:32,929`;  | Consumers of the shim: requires and the `big-carrier?` call sites (review A6).                   |\n| `yin/vm/engine.cljc:38,810`; `yin/vm/values.cljc:30,196`;    |                                                                                                  |\n| `yin/vm/data.cljc:41,421,547`; `test/yang/python/antlr/`     |                                                                                                  |\n| `{int_heap,int_literal,int_ops}_test.cljc`                   |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:1-160`                               | Hash libraries; sentinel object.                                                                 |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1-60,95-254,342-460,525-560,`   | Reader; big-integer helpers; `Float64`/`Rational`/`Decimal` carriers with equality and hash      |\n| `733-784,1070,1177`                                          | arms; float bits; `equiv-hash`; byte-ordered set and map sorting.                                |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc:1-60`;                   | Wire vocabulary; `:cljd nil` gating.                                                             |\n| `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd` (107 lines)               |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:1-40,155-260,338`               | Framing; the eight file operations; JVM `locking`.                                               |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/space/store/fs.cljc:1-60,77-224,233,265-314,`  | Claim-file algorithm (`:111-161`), `live-pid?`, `canonical-dir`, `held`, `host-lock!`, the       |\n| `351-378,419-448`                                            | `claimed` race hook, directory sync, temp nonce, platform check.                                 |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/stream/journal/file.cljc:1-70`;                | The journal over the content file under the lock; durability declaration.                        |\n| `docs/design/dao.stream.journal.md:1-25`                     |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:28-182`;                      | 12 public functions; `parallelSort`; `AtomicBoolean`; weak/soft references and the strong        |\n| `src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:87-119,182-245`;               | default; the promise facade.                                                                     |\n| `src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc:218-227`               |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/values.cljc:39-119,196`                     | `Closure`/`Continuation` equality, hash, print arms.                                             |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc:1-70,79,120`               | Ed25519 libraries; portable assembly; `SecureRandom` seeds.                                      |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:70-180`; `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:`  | Code units; NFC seam, Unicode 16.0 owner-settled; byte-ordered map/set encoding.                 |\n| `40-50,241,310-321,855-885,921,929`                          |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/stream/datagram.cljc:1-70`;                    | Seam contract; JVM receiver thread inside the seam.                                              |\n| `src/clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj:1-50`                   |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc`; `src/clj/yin/repl/host/jvm.clj`| `{:connect! :bind! :unbind!}` seam value.                                                        |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:122,621-652`                    | Attachment id by `random-uuid`; `accept-connection!` takes `now`.                                |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj:1-40`                 | Sleep machinery as host policy; the nudge/sleep pattern.                                         |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing/content/async.cljc:1-155`                 | `default-schedule`; the self-rescheduling pump.                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:132-151`                        | Seam selection by arm; `secure-random-bytes`.                                                    |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc:1-76`;               | Stage between streams; grammar profile; the one `println`.                                       |\n| `src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj:1-40`;                           |                                                                                                  |\n| `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/repl.cljc:71`                    |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `rg` over the closure for `random-uuid`, `rand`, `gensym`,   | The §3.2 census; every hit opened.                                                               |\n| clocks, ambient properties, printing, timers, `locking`,     |                                                                                                  |\n| weak references, `sort`/`compare`/`hash`, `format`           |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `test/resources/dao/jing/{cbor-v1.json,cbor-v1.errata.md,`   | Corpus and errata; fixtures; sign vectors; integer fixtures; CPython int contract.               |\n| `digest-table.edn}`; `test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures*.cljc`;    |                                                                                                  |\n| `test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn`;                       |                                                                                                  |\n| `test/yin/vm/integer_v3_fixtures.cljc`;                      |                                                                                                  |\n| `test/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_fixtures.cljc`          |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `git log` (`8351e639`, `eacaed90`); `docs/orchestrator-log.` | Codec C0, C1 landed; R lane counts.                                                              |\n| `md:11739,12375`                                             |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `collab/1792412960461-engineer-jolt-yin-repl-spike.claude-   | R: 86 of 91 namespaces loaded on a fourth host; storage not exercised.                           |\n| opus-5-5.findings.md`                                        |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.abi.md; sed -n '29,55p' docs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md; sed -n '658,790p' docs/design/dao.stream.codec.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.abi.md; sed -n '29,55p' docs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md; sed -n '658,790p' docs/design/dao.stream.codec.md\"","aggregated_output":"# datom.world ABI: the one abstraction boundary\n\nStatus: design/doctrine; owner rulings, 2026-10-19.\n\n[dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md) owns **what** the contract is: operations,\nexhaustive outcome maps, canonical values, batch and observer semantics. This\ndocument owns **why** that contract is the only boundary: role, rulings,\nimplementation taxonomy, floor and custody. It does not redefine the operations.\n\nProvenance (2026-10-19): distilled from this session's owner rulings and moved\nfrom datom.world.md, Host Boundaries. The five-round wasm consensus lineage\nis recorded in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md); subsequent owner rulings below\nsupersede its former service-protocol framing. This is the single doctrine home.\n\nAuthorities:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): master axioms and commitments.\n- [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the abstraction contract.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): execution, custody and conformance method.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): concrete machine state and fixtures.\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n## 1. The system ABI\n\nOne abstraction boundary (`dao.stream`), many\nimplementations (ring-buffer, websocket, UDP, wasm imports). `dao.stream` is\nthe contract for datom.world and `yin.vm`: operations, exhaustive outcome maps,\ncanonical values, append-only batch semantics and observer discipline. It is\nnot a transport implementation or a wire protocol. The wasm import-surface\nimplementation is specified once in [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md);\nits canonical Jing CBOR representation does not define the abstraction itself.\n\n## 2. Implementations and the taxonomy ruling\n\n**Owner clarification, 2026-10-19 (verbatim):** “dao.stream is an abstraction\nboundary. It is not a transport implementation. It can be implemented using a\nring-buffer, a websocket or UDP”\n\nThe wasm import surface is another implementation: adapters implement the\nabstraction through imported functions over linear memory. A co-resident\nproducer can instead use an in-memory ring-buffer implementation inside the\ninstance with zero host crossings. Both preserve the same abstraction contract.\n\n## 3. Custody at the boundary\n\nWhat crosses a trust domain is a strict subset of in-machine values: closures\nand continuations cross only as admissible portable plain data; host types,\nresources and code artifacts never do. The custody gates retain this distinction\n([yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md)); an implementation grants no additional\nportability or authority.\n\n## 4. Boundary ruling and supersession\n\n**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found;\none abstraction boundary is the principle.** The boundary between wasm and datom.world is\n`dao.stream`; its implementation uses whatever wasm exposes to the outside\nworld. The exposed functions are **adapters to dao.stream**, which is the ABI\nto `yin.vm` and the whole ecosystem. Machine-internal work executes inside the\nwasm instance: the Rust engine crates and compiled register bodies share memory\nand direct calls. Only effects/facts cross through the stream adapters. World\nfacts still require interaction with the world; moving engine implementation\ninside the machine does not manufacture those facts.\n\n**Dated supersession (owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19):** This replaces\nthe earlier same-day “sole justification … performance” allowance for\nprocess-local services and the service-protocol interpretation of\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`). Its private operation vocabulary, TLV codec,\nexpanded-canonical-bytes comparator and service-status protocol are dissolved.\nPerformance work optimizes internal execution or the stream implementation; it does\nnot reintroduce a semantic service interface. [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md)\nis the compiler implementation plan for this in-instance architecture.\n\n## 5. Naming and the floor\n\n“The system ABI” names only `dao.stream`. Earlier qualified guest-host service\nnames are historical provenance for the dissolved protocol, not names for a\nsecond contract. Implementation specs name their implementation (WebSocket,\nwasm import surface), never “the wasm ABI”. Internal compiler linkage does not\ncreate an ecosystem boundary.\n\nThe floor is the need to obtain world facts that shipped code cannot compute.\nThat seam is immovable; the engineering frontier is movable: machine-internal\noperations can move inside the instance without changing the abstraction.\nAn in-instance stream between co-resident producers and observers needs no host\ncrossing. Changing physical placement does not remove external facts or custody.\n\nImplementations include ring-buffer, [WebSocket](./dao.stream.ws.md),\n[wasm imports](./dao.stream.wasm.md), and UDP. These implement one abstraction;\nnone adds an ABI. The import-surface representation and buffer rules belong\nonly to its implementation spec.\n\n## 6. Effects out and events in\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n## 7. Adapter discipline\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## 1. Imported adapter surface\n\nUse import module `dao.stream` with the following proposed import names.\nAll parameters and single results are i32. `p,n` address one canonical Jing\nCBOR value; `out,cap` denote a disjoint writable response region. The returned\ni32 is the **encoded response byte length**, never a status number. Decode\nthat response into the operation's exhaustive outcome map. Zero-copy views\nmust not outlive the synchronous call or memory growth.\n\n| Import | Parameters → result | Existing stream operation |\n|---|---|---|\n| `create` | `(p,n,out,cap) → length` | `create!` on a creation specification (VM make-stream adapter). |\n| `attach` | `(p,n,out,cap) → length` | `attach!` on a portable descriptor. |\n| `descriptor` | `(handle,out,cap) → length` | `descriptor`. |\n| `cursor` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `cursor` with decoded anchor. |\n| `next` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `next` with decoded cursor; the poll operation. |\n| `append` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `append!` with decoded value. |\n| `close` | `(handle,out,cap) → length` | `close!`. |\n\nMake/poll are VM-facing terminology, not additional stream operations. Park,\nresume, resolve-var, primitive application, state reads, roots, collection and\nmodule handling have **no imports**: they execute in-instance. Imports never\nname arbitrary FFI operations. Memory/table provision and module instantiation\nare runtime linkage/lifecycle mechanics, not semantic service operations.\n\nOperational stream handles cannot themselves be canonical data. The import-surface implementation\nrepresents the handle field in a successful create/attach outcome by a checked,\n## 7. Migration\n\n### 7.1 Slices: expand, migrate, contract\n\nReview finding A2 showed the earlier sequence could not land green slice by\nslice: removing defaults and renaming keys in one step while `remote-channel`,\nthe host adapters and `dao.jing.stream` still read the old keys would have\nbroken production compositions and turned Jing's name test into a nil-to-nil\ncomparison that selects the CBOR branch for Transit. The sequence is\nrestructured as expand (add, nothing removed), migrate (callers switch),\ncontract (remove). Each slice lands on master alone, under the three-lane\nquick tier, with recorded test counts before and after and with the frozen\nwire fixtures of §7.3 passing. \"Counts unchanged\" means the same number of\ntests and assertions per lane, with any added test listed by name.\n\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Slice | Phase    | Change                                                                      | Falsifiable acceptance                                              |\n+=======+==========+=============================================================================+=====================================================================+\n| F0    | freeze   | Record the frozen wire fixtures (§7.3) from the tree **before** any other   | Fixture files committed; the fixture test passes on all three       |\n|       |          | slice, at a tagged master commit. No `src/` change.                         | lanes against unchanged code; the tagged commit is named in the     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | slice report as the \"old peer\" for §7.3's bidirectional check.      |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C0    | expand   | Create `dao.stream.codec`: accessors with dual lookup, `profile?` accepting | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus the named new tests in     |\n|       |          | either complete key set, `safe-number?` and bounds moved from Transit, the  | `codec_test.cljc`: the §4.4 law corpus over both profiles **minus   |\n|       |          | D predicate with `validate-portable`, `host-bytes?` (moved from             | the baseline exception paths of §7.3** (today: Transit lists, which |\n|       |          | `cbor/byte-payload?`, which stays as a forwarding def). Both profiles gain  | round-trip on CBOR and are asserted to keep their frozen per-host   |\n|       |          | the six `:dao.stream.codec/*` keys **beside** the five `:ws/*` keys.        | Transit outcome; §4.4 ruling R2), the adversarial identifier and    |\n|       |          | `dao.stream.cbor`, `dao.stream.transit` and `dao.data` require the          | metadata cases (collection and symbol metadata, direct and nested), |\n|       |          | contract; `cbor.cljc` drops its Transit require. No consumer changes.       | the dual-key gate. `transit-domain-stays-a-subset-of-the-cbor-      |\n|       |          | Transit's decoder is **not** touched, even where it breaks the law (R2).    | domain` is replaced by the law test (its claim was false in         |\n|       |          |                                                                             | general, §4.4). `grep dao.stream.transit src/` no longer hits       |\n|       |          |                                                                             | `cbor.cljc` or `data.cljc`. Fixtures pass. No behaviour change      |\n|       |          |                                                                             | anywhere.                                                           |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C1    | expand   | Consumers read profiles only through the accessors, keeping every default:  | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus: `jing/stream_test`        |\n|       |          | `dao.stream.ws` (gate, `codec-index`, `distinct?` check, frame kind,        | cases for nil, `{}` and a **malformed** profile (a key missing,     |\n|       |          | encode/decode calls), the four host adapters (negotiation and the           | `bytes?` absent or non-boolean, a non-token name), all rejected     |\n|       |          | subprotocol they put on the socket), `dao.jing.stream` (`profile?` then     | before any lane branch; and, positively, a **complete profile       |\n|       |          | `bytes?`; drops both codec requires). `remote-channel` **accepts**          | under an unfamiliar name** (a stub codec the test defines, never    |\n|       |          | optional `:codecs`/`:codec` and threads them to endpoint, bind and          | Transit's or CBOR's name) run twice, with `bytes?` `true` and with  |\n|       |          | attacher; absent, the `ws` default still applies.                           | `bytes?` `false`, asserting the bytes branch and the octets branch  |\n|       |          |                                                                             | respectively, so dispatch is proved to be by capability and not by  |\n|       |          |                                                                             | name (A2-r2); then the Transit and CBOR profiles as today. `grep    |\n|       |          |                                                                             | ':ws/\\(subprotocol\\|frame-kind\\|portable-value?\\|encode\\|decode\\)'` |\n|       |          |                                                                             | over `src/` hits only the two profiles, the seam key sites, and     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | `codec.cljc`'s fallback. A `remote-channel` spec naming a codec     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | is honoured end to end (one test per host). Fixtures pass.          |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C2    | migrate  | Every caller supplies its profile explicitly: `yin.repl.serve`/`connect`    | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus the fixture-driven         |\n|       |          | pass `dao.stream.transit/profile`; every test in §7.2 names its profile;    | tests; `grep` for codec-less `make-attacher`/`make-endpoint`/       |\n|       |          | one parameterized fixture runs codec-neutral suites over both profiles;     | `listen!`/serve/dial calls over `src/` and `test/` returns none.    |\n|       |          | one-arity descriptor gates replaced by two-arity calls at every site.       | The §7.3 bidirectional old/new peer check runs here for both        |\n|       |          | Defaults still exist but nothing reaches them.                              | profiles. Fixtures pass.                                            |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C3    | contract | `dao.stream.ws` drops the Transit require, `checked-codec`'s default, the   | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus: codec-less                |\n|       |          | one-arity gates, the `make-endpoint` default and the no-offer default       | `make-attacher`, `make-endpoint`, `listen!`/`handle-request!`       |\n|       |          | (refused). The four adapters drop `default-codecs`, the Transit require     | throw (one test per host); a seam without a name is refused;        |\n|       |          | and the `ws/subprotocol` fallback. `remote-channel` refuses `::no-codec`.   | `remote-channel` returns `::no-codec`. `grep dao.stream.transit     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | src/` hits only `transit*.clj*` and `yin/repl/{serve,connect}`.     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | `dao.stream.ws.md` \"absent name\" sentence amended; its              |\n|       |          |                                                                             | codec-profile text points here. Fixtures pass.                      |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C4    | contract | Contract dual acceptance to one key set. Runs only after OQ3 is resolved    | Three lanes green; counts unchanged except the dual-key gate        |\n|       |          | (§10), and which set survives is OQ3's answer. **If OQ3 moves the keys**    | tests of C0, which are **rewritten** (not deleted) in this slice:   |\n|       |          | (CD5): both profiles drop the `:ws/*` keys, accessors become single lookup, | each test that asserted \"a map complete under the other key set is  |\n|       |          | `profile?` accepts the `:dao.stream.codec/*` set only. **If OQ3 keeps       | accepted\" now asserts it is rejected, each test that asserted the   |\n|       |          | `:ws/*`**: both profiles drop the `:dao.stream.codec/*` keys, the dual      | fallback lookup now asserts a single lookup, and the count of       |\n|       |          | lookup is deleted, the accessors read `:ws/*`, `profile?` accepts the five  | gate tests before and after is equal and recorded. The grep gate    |\n|       |          | `:ws/*` keys plus one added `:ws/bytes?` boolean (the capability of §3.3    | is **conditional on OQ3**: under the move, `grep ':ws/\\(frame-kind  |\n|       |          | must survive either answer).                                                | \\|portable-value?\\|encode\\|decode\\|bytes?\\)'` over `src/` and       |\n|       |          |                                                                             | `test/` is empty and `:ws/subprotocol` survives only on seam maps;  |\n|       |          |                                                                             | under keep, `grep ':dao.stream.codec/'` over `src/` and `test/`     |\n|       |          |                                                                             | hits only the namespace's own name, and no absence of `:ws/*` is    |\n|       |          |                                                                             | asserted. Under both answers: fixtures pass; the §7.3 bidirectional |\n|       |          |                                                                             | check runs again. Slow tier once.                                   |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| C5    | owner    | Owner-gated (§6.4): `yin.repl` selects `dao.stream.cbor/profile`;           | Same suites green over CBOR; `grep dao.stream.transit src/` hits    |\n|       |          | cross-host peer suites configured for CBOR.                                 | only `transit*.clj*`. Not commissioned by this document.            |\n+-------+----------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nWhy each intermediate state is green. After C0 every consumer still reads\n`:ws/*` keys that are still present. After C1 every consumer reads through\naccessors that find either key set, and every default is still in place, so\na composition that names nothing behaves exactly as today. After C2 nothing\nreaches a default, so C3 can delete the defaults without changing any\nobservable path; C3 is the only slice whose acceptance includes a behaviour\nchange (codec-less compositions fail), and that change is confined to direct\ncompositions and tests, which C2 already migrated. C4 deletes keys nobody\nreads. The dependency is strictly linear: F0, C0, C1, C2, C3, C4.\n\nF0 through C4 depend on nothing outside this document. Other tracks may depend\non them (any track that needs `dao.stream.ws` free of a codec default); such a\ndependency is stated in the other track's document, never here.\n\n### 7.2 Test impact (V, grep of `test/` on 2026-10-20)\n\nNamespaces requiring `dao.stream.transit`: 22 by `grep -l dao.stream.transit\ntest/` (an earlier brief said 20; the grep count is authoritative):\n`dao.stream.ws-test`,\n`ws-codec-test` (`.cljc`, `.cljd`), `ws.jvm-test`, `ws.node-test`,\n`ws.browser-test`, `ws-project-peer`, `ws-project-cross-jvm-test`,\n`ws-project-cross-node-test`, `remote-route-test`, `loopback-net`,\n`transit-test`, `cbor-test` (`.cljc`, `.cljd`), `dao.jing.stream-test`,\n`yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `head-board-test`,\n`transfer-peer`, `yin.repl.main-test`, `yin.repl.slice-peer`. They keep\nrequiring the codec they exercise; that is composition.\n\nNamespaces that rely on a default and change in C2: `ws-test`\n(`make-attacher` without `:codec` at `:41,68,93,110,133,157,186`; `make-endpoint`\nwithout `:codecs` at `:207,262,521`; seams without a name at\n`:224-234,243,272,304,336,427,532-533,583`; one-arity gates at `:567-570`),\n`ws-codec-test` (both), `ws.jvm-test`, `ws.node-test`, `ws.dart-test`,\n`ws.browser-test`, `ws-project-test`, `ws-project-jvm-test`, `ws-project-peer`,\n`ws-project-cross-jvm-test`, `ws-project-cross-node-test`,\n`remote-channel-test` (`ws/descriptor?` one-arity at `:174`; serve/dial\nwithout codec), `loopback-net` (no seam name; Transit at `:164`),\n`yin.repl.host.jvm-test` (`:87-109`), and the yin.repl network tests through\nserve/connect. `transit-test` keeps its predicate cases (`transit_test.cljc:15-57`)\nunchanged, since Transit's predicate is unchanged; `codec-test` is new.\n`cbor-test` loses `transit-domain-stays-a-subset-of-the-cbor-domain` (both\nhosts) to the law test in C0 and keeps the rest; `journal-test`\n(`cbor/validate-portable`, `:354`) is unaffected. Exact per-lane counts are\nthe slice reports' to record.\n\n### 7.3 Wire proof: frozen fixtures and bidirectional peers\n\nReview finding A3: the cross-host peer suites run new code against new code,\nso both ends can drift together and \"wire unchanged\" would be asserted, not\nproved. The proof has two parts, both mandatory.\n\n**Frozen fixtures (F0).** Generated once from the pre-change tree at the\ntagged F0 commit and committed under `test/` as data files the three lanes\nall load (one directory per profile; CBOR payloads as hex, Transit payloads\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
