tarts at the §7.8 minimum baseline. §7.8 on both platforms: packet and completeness parity,        |\n1271:| J1: numeric and content correctness         | Exact integer     | Existing numeric/CBOR/hash corpus bytes and refusals match JVM/CLJS/CLJD; official hash boundaries; |\n1316:| J7: UDP and DHT                             | Integrate the J0b | §8.4; J0b regressions retained; would-block/ENOBUFS is transport-error plus send-failed; no `full`, |\n1323:|                                             | and subsystem     | and J1-J7 gate on both platforms; existing three lanes green; same shared kit, no overlays/stubs,   |\n1333:J0a, J0b and J0c are independently commissionable and reportable; nothing is\n1335:having landed, nothing else here does, and it runs on its own schedule. J0c starts at its minimum baseline (§7.8), before unrelated\n1337:J1 depends on J0a; J5 on J0c plus the correctness and CLI slices; J7 on J0b. A\n1338:J0b or J0c failure does not erase a tracked-load result.\n1340:Every slice needs both macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 to close. A one-platform J0a\n1341:report is useful partial evidence. A macOS-first partial report for J0b/J0c is\n1347:there is part of J0a. An unavailable machine leaves the slice incomplete, not\n1384:Measurement. From J0a/J0c: cold/warm startup, first REPL latency, native parser\n1417:  J0c runs early.\n1447:| Q3         | \"Q3 is not relevant if we use FFI     | In-process ANTLR4 C++ parser over FFI. J0c required; on failure the orchestrator returns to the owner with the   |\n1485:the UDP probe to J0\" (J0b).\n1491:1. **Platform reporting.** macOS arm64 may report first on J0b/J0c, as already\n1492:   allowed for J0a. The report is marked partial; Linux x86_64 evidence is\n1494:   (J0c: to the owner, no fallback).\n1505:commission J0b\" (2026-10-19). Defaults 1 to 3 therefore take effect; they stay\n1510:to commissioning the slice that would apply it (J0a for default 3; J0b/J0c for\n1541:- Falsifiable consequences for jolt. J0a, once the codec track has landed:\n1547:  specified and the question returns to the owner; nothing in J0a-J5 changes.\n1558:  as a clean peer close, forbidden by §9.2. (iii) J0b already exercised the\n1601:**Ruling 4 (A5 wording, 2026-10-20): J0a keeps `96d7b866`; from the first\n1604:J0a row.\n1608:  all S and J0b evidence describes it; re-pinning now would discard the only\n1618:  none\", the nearest release tag and the describe string; J0a reproduces the S\n1622:- Reversal. A J0a or J1 blocker whose fix exists only in a later tag, or an\n1638:| R1      | Reuse the shared CST exporter through a jolt cst adapter; only raw ops-level data      | §7.3, §7.8, J0c                                                    |\n1641:| R2      | Stage-owned native worker, disposal, one-thread use, private simulators/DFA/caches,    | §7.4, J0c, J5                                                      |\n1644:| R3      | UDP attribution corrected; landed transport-error plus lifecycle event; no             | §8.1, §8.3, J0b, J7, Q7                                            |\n1647:| R4      | Explicit Homebrew `openssl@3` / system `libcrypto.so.3` paths and linkage, pre-digest  | §6.3, J0a                                                          |\n1650:| R5      | `:blocking` pointer calls, concurrent full-GC tests, measured stack budget and         | §6.3, §7.5, §7.6, J0c, J5 (as modified by W5)                      |\n1656:| R7      | Independently commissionable J0a/J0b/J0c; J0c early; both platforms to close           | §10.1, §10.2                                                       |\n1663:| R10     | Exact CMake pin at J0a; Q5-Q7 recorded; worker lifecycle replaces lifecycle denial     | §6.1, J0a, §13.1, §7.4                                             |\n1670:|         |                                                                                        | §13.4 ruling 1 withdrawn); J0a inventory, J2, J6, §12              |\n1680:|         |                                                                                        | acceptance item); J0a precondition                                 |\n1684:| W8      | J0c start criterion and lowering scope                                                 | §7.8 (minimum baseline; restricted lowering corpus; J1 diffs       |\n1685:|         |                                                                                        | reported separately), J0c                                          |\n> **Owner ruling, 2026-10-20 (verbatim):** \"when the host boundary can be an\n> IO monad then dao.stream is used. If it cannot be a monad than dao.stream\n> doesnt have to be used\". Asked whether same-host-ness or the IO-action test\n> governs, the owner answered \"yes, test 2 governs\".\n>\n> A host contact uses `dao.stream` if and only if it can be an IO action: its\n> result depends on something other than its arguments; it changes something\n> outside the machine's values; two equal calls may answer differently\n> (entropy, a clock, a process counter); its answer is not available when the\n> call returns; whether it completes depends on the world; or it runs in\n> another process, host or trust domain. A same-host effect (a file read, a\n> clock reading, entropy) is an IO action and so a stream.\n>\n> A contact that cannot be an IO action, because it is a pure, total,\n> synchronous function of its arguments executed inside the same instance,\n> may be a direct call. Such functions are the dialect's value model and the\n> deterministic functions over it (exact integers, bytes, text, hashing,\n> signing, canonical encoding). They are enumerated in a host primitive\n> profile with an oracle per entry ([dao.stream.host-boundary.md](./dao.stream.host-boundary.md)\n> §4.3), execute in-instance (for wasm: compiled in, never imported, §1 of\n> [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md)), and add no contract to\n> `dao.stream`. Linking a library to compute one is internal linkage (§5).\n>\n> §7's two non-adapters are read under this test: a map of functions that\n> portable application code calls to reach the world is a host service and is\n> rejected; a seam a stream implementation calls privately to perform its own\n> IO is that implementation's adapter and is not.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
