{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '37,45p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '475,493p;574,616p;770,787p'; sed -n '30,55p' src/cljc/dao/jing/content/driver.clj; rg -n 'content.coordinate|jing.coordinate|coordinate :as' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc; sed -n '831,840p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\"","aggregated_output":";; Settings (§3.1)\n\n(def ^:private default-ref-type\n  ;; §5.3: :soft on the JVM (memory-pressure-aware), :strong on cljs/cljd\n  ;; where :soft would silently degrade to :weak.\n  #?(:cljd :strong\n     :clj :soft\n     :cljs :strong))\n\n   475\t+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n   476\t| `write-text-atomically!`| `fs.cljc:352-378` (temp, fsync, rename)                                    | `{:ok}` / `{:failed stage}` where stage names write, sync or  |\n   477\t| dir name text           |                                                                            | rename; a `:failed` leaves HEAD as it was (rename atomicity). |\n   478\t+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n   479\t| `sync-dir!` path        | `fs.cljc:292-314`                                                          | `{:ok}` / `{:failed}` / `{:unsupported}`; declared as           |\n   480\t|                         |                                                                            | `:directory-sync?` in the seam's capability map.              |\n   481\t+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n   482\t| `read-text` path        | `fs.cljc:77-84`, `store.cljc:159`                                          | `{:ok text}` / `{:absent}` / `{:failed}`.                      |\n   483\t+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n   484\t| `ensure-dir!` path      | `journal/file.cljc:55-61`                                                  | `{:ok}` / `{:failed}`.                                        |\n   485\t+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n   486\t| Capability map          | `journal/file.cljc:36-41` (durability declaration)                         | `{:lock-kind :os-lock|:claim-file, :directory-sync? bool,      |\n   487\t|                         |                                                                            | :failure-model :process-crash|:power-loss}`.                  |\n   488\t+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n   489\t\n   490\tPortable and written once: framing and torn-tail recovery (F1-F6), the\n   491\tclaim-file algorithm and its pid-reuse refusal, the same-process `held`\n   492\trecord, the HEAD protocol, replay order. The temp-file name's nonce\n   493\t(`fs.cljc:351`) and the claim nonce (`:137`) come from the entropy stream\n   574\t| `locking` (JVM), atoms,          | Five `locking` sites (§3.2 row 7); single-owner discipline elsewhere.                               | none  |\n   575\t| volatiles                        |                                                                                                     |       |\n   576\t+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n   577\t| Host promise type                | `btree/storage.cljc:218-227`: becomes driver-side composition code in H5 (table 3.1).              | H5    |\n   578\t+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n   579\t| Dialect syntax                   | §3.3.                                                                                               | H0    |\n   580\t+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------+\n   581\t\n   582\tLayering: `dao.host.*` requires nothing in the repo. `dao.jing`,\n   583\t`dao.jing.cbor`, `yin.vm.integer`, `yin.vm.data`, `yin.vm.values`,\n   584\t`yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm` and the three Python integer tests require it;\n   585\t`yin.vm.integer.host` is deleted only when `grep yin.vm.integer.host src/\n   586\ttest/` is empty (H2; review A6). `dao` never requires `yin`.\n   587\t\n   588\t### 4.4 Entropy, clock and scheduling as stream data; the driver\n   589\t\n   590\t**Entropy (P).** One `dao.stream.entropy` reader: a ring-buffer stream the\n   591\tdriver's seam deposits CSPRNG bytes onto, in fixed-size batches, and refills\n   592\twhen a lifecycle event reports consumption. Portable code holds a cursor on\n   593\tit like any stream and draws bytes with `next`; an empty entropy stream is\n   594\t`:dao.stream/blocked`, answered as data, never waited on. Fresh identities,\n   595\tsecrets, nonces, request ids and key seeds are **derived from drawn bytes**\n   596\tby a portable function (`dao.stream.entropy/fresh-id`, a hex string of 16\n   597\tdrawn bytes). Creation specifications carry `{:identity id}`; `create!` no\n   598\tlonger mints. In tests the \"driver\" is a fixture that deposits a fixed byte\n   599\tsequence, which makes identities reproducible, a property no test has today.\n   600\tThis is \"events in\" (`abi.md` §6): the host initiates nothing and invokes\n   601\tnothing; the deposit is the adapter's. No portable code calls a generator.\n   602\t\n   603\t**Clock.** Unchanged: a reading is data the driver passes (`now`) or deposits\n   604\t(a tick event). The contract lists it as the driver's duty.\n   605\t\n   606\t**Scheduling (P, review A2).** `dao.jing.content.async` loses\n   607\t`default-schedule` and its self-rescheduling pump. `async-content` becomes a\n   608\tstepped owner with a `step!` the driver calls on its own cadence (the\n   609\t`waitset.driver` sleep/nudge pattern, `driver.clj:1-14`, V, already models\n   610\tthis); callbacks owed to consumers fire inside `step!`. `btree.storage`'s\n   611\tpromise facade (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`) moves to driver-side\n   612\tcomposition code that steps and resolves, or its consumers step directly;\n   613\tthe portable namespace keeps only the stepped functions. `dao.space.dht`\n   614\tdrops `secure-random-bytes` and reads the entropy stream for node ids and\n   615\tnonces; `default-bind` stays a seam selection (the datagram seam value the\n   616\tcomposition passes).\n   770\t`dao.space.index`, `dao.space.query` and the UCF are untouched;\n   771\t`yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm` and `yin.vm.values` are touched by a require (H2).\n   772\tTests: about 10^3 rerun per lane (R: JVM quick tier about 4,000; `dao.stream`\n   773\tand `dao.jing` subsystems about 690 per lane), about 10^1 rewritten (those\n   774\tthat construct streams, sign keys or storage directly, and `integer_test`'s\n   775\tshim cases), about 10^1 added. No performance figure is claimed; H4 measures.\n   776\t\n   777\tWhat would reverse the design:\n   778\t- An owner ruling that reader-conditional arms are the intended port\n   779\t  mechanism. Cheaper per port, no enumeration, no oracle per entry, a\n   780\t  library requirement per host.\n   781\t- A measured H4 regression that matters. The portable writer stays as the\n   782\t  oracle and a host supplies a faster one behind the same profile.\n   783\t- A host whose lock or durability semantics the §4.2 seam cannot express as\n   784\t  data: it declares the capability absent and compositions refuse durable\n   785\t  storage there; the seam shape stands.\n   786\t- A decision that identities may stay host-minted. Then H5 shrinks to the\n   787\t  secure-generator sites and reproducible identities are not promised.\nsed: src/cljc/dao/jing/content/driver.clj: No such file or directory\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n\n### 11.1 Owner decisions recorded (2026-10-20)\n\nThe owner adopted all six r1 recommendations: \"agree with all of fable's\nrecommendations\"; and for OQ5 separately: \"makes sense. i agree with fable's\nrecommendation\".\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| Id   | Decision as adopted                                                                                              | Status                       |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"agent_message","text":"**NOT SIGNED OFF.**\n\nRead all 1,021 lines, both reviews, doctrine, and repository evidence, including more than a dozen cited source/oracle files. No edits.\n\n`H` = [dao.stream.host-boundary.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md). Source paths below are relative to `src/`.\n\n- **A1 — Resolved for placement:** §§2.3, 2.4, 7 correctly require in-instance primitives and acknowledge porting beyond streams. ABI §§4–5 and wasm’s seven imports support this; no second ABI is introduced. The operational definition still needs correction below.\n- **A2 — Partly resolved:** §§4.1/4.4 remove callable entropy generators and timer thunks, but explicitly retain consumer callbacks inside `step!`.\n- **A3 — Partly resolved:** §3.2 lists **26** in-scope UUID calls, not 24/25. The 27th is excluded `dao/postgraphics/terminal.cljc:14`. No listed UUID site is missing; counts and broader closure claims are wrong.\n- **A4 — Partly resolved:** §4.3 genuinely enumerates 66 functions, but omits carrier operations and migration assignments; runtime prerequisites do not satisfy the stated final gate.\n- **A5 — Partly resolved:** Aliasing, malformed UTF-8, Unicode version, stability and corpus exceptions are improved. Fixtures exist, but grouped suites are not an entry-to-case coverage matrix. Base64 tests already exist; integer/sign suites contain 25/4 deftests, not 26/5.\n- **A6 — Partly resolved:** H2 names all eight external shim consumers found, including three Python tests. Its pre-deletion empty-grep condition still matches the shim’s own namespace; H1’s direct tests are correctly required.\n- **A7 — Partly resolved:** §4.2 supplies the missing claim-file operations, but its atomic-replacement failure guarantee is unsound.\n- **A8 — Partly resolved:** §§8–11 remove invented performance numbers and record OQ1–OQ6. Cost, risk and file-disjointness remain understated.\n\n| ID | Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | High | H:169,224,872 | “Equal calls differ” classifies weak-reference reads as IO, then exempts them. Mutable byte/array entries also contradict the profile’s blanket purity claim. | Define the boundary first, distinguish owned runtime state from external facts, and specify mutation/ownership laws. Preserve §12 as an owner proposal; do not present this inconsistent definition as verbatim doctrine. |\n| R2 | Blocking | H:590–610 | Stream cursors are non-destructive (`cljc/dao/stream.cljc:159–173`): independent readers can reuse entropy. No allocation, partial-batch, loss or blocked-resumption protocol is specified. `fresh-id`’s 32 hex characters fail `ucf/custody.cljc:50–56`’s UUID grammar. DHT initialization draws multiple values synchronously (`space/dht.cljc:255–269`). | Specify exclusive allocation and resumable consumption, bootstrap identities, preserved UUID spelling, and DHT failure/continuation behavior. Emit async completions as data; moving callbacks into `step!` does not remove them. |\n| R3 | High | H:318–358 | This is lexical grep, not resolved closure analysis. `jing/coordinate.cljc:80` selects a blocking driver whose `clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj:169–206` reads clocks and sleeps. `sign.cljc:79` is deterministic seed replay, not fresh entropy. Regex also appears in custody/datagram. | Commit a transitive, lane-specific closure with classifications; correct 27 total/26 scoped UUID counts and all residual assignments. |\n| R4 | High | H:507–579 | Missing operations include JVM Decimal/Ratio construction/access (`jing/cbor.cljc:483–632`). `yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:232–245,315` retains float/BigInteger interop without a migration assignment. Btree’s actual default is JVM `:soft` (`btree.cljc:39–44`), not universally strong. | Complete the manifest/runtime mapping and assign migrations. Reconcile retained runtime arms with H0’s final allowlist and impossible H2 greps. |\n| R5 | High | H:476–477 | `store/fs.cljc:367–385` renames before directory sync; sync failure can leave new HEAD visible. | Distinguish pre-rename failure, replacement visibility and durability uncertainty; test post-rename failure explicitly. |\n| R6 | High | H:744–775 | Mandatory identities require edits to codec-owned tests: `ws_test.cljc:11`, `remote_route_test.cljc:24` construct identity-less rings. §9 also says UCF untouched despite H5 custody/holder migrations. | Allocate source **and test** files, provide staged compatibility or dependencies, and re-estimate H5. Base64 replacement and new refusal laws are not verbatim code motion. |\n\nJ1 arm-adding should wait. J0b/J0c probes can proceed; J0a is **not wholly unaffected**—its source adaptations require coordination.\n\nOQ7/OQ8 appropriately seek owner decisions. OQ9 alternatives both can avoid core generators; its recommendation overclaims exclusivity. OQ10 may ask about accepting a residual, but compiler counters need hygienic naming, not merely resetting numbering; transaction `gensym` needs separate treatment.\n\nNot verified: executable lane parity, performance, crash/race behavior, exhaustive resolved closure/oracle coverage, or produced wasm imports. These remain proposed gates, not demonstrated properties."}}
